# Development Process

A broad-strokes map of how work flows, faithful to the developer's flowchart
(replicated in `process-flowchart.md` at the repo root). The terms used here
(stages, phases, pathways, roles) are defined in `definitions.mdc`; the detailed
behavior lives in the rules cross-referenced below — this file maps the flow, it
does not restate them.

Work enters as **intake**, runs through two stages in order — **Action Stage →
Git Stage** — then post-deployment verification. Every "not ready", "more
changes", or failed check routes **back to intake** (a failed test result is
just new user input).

## The process is mandatory

This flow is the **required** route, not a sketch of the happy path. Phases and
stages run **in order**, and each gate is answered before the work moves past
it.

**Only the developer waives a step.** An **explicit** developer instruction to
skip, shortcut, reorder, or bypass a phase, stage, or gate is honored, and is
reported as a deviation (see `core.mdc` "Developer Overrides";
`human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`). Absent such an instruction the
process is not optional — an agent must never:

- **Self-authorize a shortcut** on its own judgment that a step isn't needed,
  the change looks fine, tests are overkill, or it would be faster.
- **Pick the lighter pathway, or call work trivial, to reduce process** —
  dodging planning, the auditor, or unit tests (see
  `development/parallel-subagents.mdc`, `development/independent-review.mdc`,
  `testing/unit-testing.mdc`).
- **Answer a developer gate itself.** **Plan ready?**, **Ready?** after user
  testing, **More changes?**, **Passing and ready to merge?**, and the final
  **Passing?** after end-user testing are the developer's calls: ask, then
  wait.
- **Work around a failed check.** A failing test, check, or audit routes **back
  to intake** — it is never suppressed, weakened, disabled, or papered over.

Keeping completed work committed locally is required regardless of where the
gates stand (see `git/always-commit-work.mdc`); a commit is not a claim that
the **Ready?** gate cleared.

## Intake

A feature idea or GitHub issue becomes **user input** (a feature request *or a
test result*). Creating a GitHub issue is optional — issues are not the only
source of work. A single decision, **determine nature of request**, picks the
pathway:

- **Large feature** (many files / multi-domain) → **plan-based**.
- **Bugfix or small, localized feature** → **one-shot**.

When the request doesn't state a pathway, infer it, then state and confirm the
choice with the developer before proceeding — see
`development/parallel-subagents.mdc` ("Confirm the pathway when unspecified").

## Action Stage

Where code is written, tested, and debugged.

### Plan-based (larger features)

Planning → Development → Testing.

- **Planning** — an **organizer** handles user clarifications and writes the plan, gated by a **Plan ready?** check (see `planning/plans.mdc`, `roles/organizer.mdc`).
- **Development** — the organizer implements the plan with one or many parallel **doers**; an **auditor** checks their work against the plan, gated by a **Plan implemented properly?** check (see `development/parallel-subagents.mdc`, `roles/organizer.mdc`, `roles/doer.mdc`, `roles/auditor.mdc`; review gate: `development/independent-review.mdc`). Doers write unit tests for their modules.
- **Testing** — unit tests run, gated by a **Passing?** check that loops back to Development on failure (see `testing/irl-testing.mdc`).

### One-shot (small / localized change)

No Planning phase.

- **Development** — after any needed clarification, a single **lone agent** implements the feature and its unit tests (see `roles/lone-agent.mdc`).
- **Testing** — unit tests run, gated by a **Passing?** check that loops back to the lone agent on failure (see `testing/irl-testing.mdc`).

### Converge

Both pathways end by summarizing the work
(outcomes/findings/changes/deviations/follow-ups) into the CHANGELOG scratchpad
(see `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`; wrap-up: `human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`), then
**user testing** and a **Ready?** gate. Not ready → back to intake; ready →
Git Stage.

## Git Stage

Commit, push, PR, checks, merge, release.

1. **Commit** to the local working branch; a **More changes?** gate can send work back to intake (see `git/always-commit-work.mdc`, `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`).
2. **Push** to the remote working branch and **open/update the PR** with issue references and action-item checkboxes; the coordinator clears the CHANGELOG scratchpad once at this push step (see `git/git-branch-and-pr-workflow.mdc`, `git/pull-request.mdc`, `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`).
3. **GitHub actions checks** (gated by a **Passing?** check) then **action-item testing** (gated by a **Passing and ready to merge?** check); failures route back to intake.
4. **Merge into main and create a release** (see `git/git-branch-and-pr-workflow.mdc`, `git/pull-request.mdc`).

## Post-deployment

Deployment → **end-user testing** → a final **Passing?** gate: pass is **done**;
failure loops back to intake. Human-only (in-real-life) verification is
developer-confirmed — never claimed by an agent (see `testing/irl-testing.mdc`).

## Diagram

Simplified spine (the full replica, with per-node roles and colors, is in
`process-flowchart.md`):

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Intake["Intake: user input (feature request or test result)"] --> Determine{"Determine nature of request"}
    Determine -- "Large feature" --> Plan["Plan-based: Planning → Development → Testing"]
    Determine -- "Bugfix / small" --> One["One-shot: Development → Testing"]
    Plan --> Converge["Summarize + CHANGELOG → user testing → Ready?"]
    One --> Converge
    Converge -- "Yes" --> Git["Git Stage: commit → push/PR → checks → merge/release"]
    Converge -- "No" --> Intake
    Git --> Post["Deployment → end-user testing → Passing?"]
    Post -- "Yes" --> Done(["Done!"])
    Post -- "No" --> Intake
    Git -. "any failed check" .-> Intake
```

